{quote}I think it would be more efficient to get some feedback from you on some of the items that I find risky and we can decide collectively if they are worth pushing back on/amending or you may already be happy to accept these as is. I don’t want to cut across any commercial negotiations that you have had.

# Article I currently defines the Indemnity Agreement as a single, continuous Program covering all renewals. What this means:

* Any renewal or change automatically falls under the same agreement, even if you don’t sign a new one.
* A problem in one policy year (for example, a late payment or a dispute) could be treated as a default across the entire Program.
* The insurer could argue that the Letter of Credit and obligations stay in force until all years and all claims are fully closed.{quote}



{color:#ff5630}DR: I think this is largely fine. {color}

{quote}

# Insurer controls coverage, amounts, and we must “follow the fortunes” (Article IV). Aviva decides what is “Loss,” whether it’s covered, and how much to pay; we’re bound by Aviva’s judgment and must “follow the fortunes and settlements.” This can force reimbursement of disputed payments/settlements within the Self-Insured Retention (SIR). The alternative is to require prior written consent for any settlement or payment within the Retention (or above a threshold), “consent not to be unreasonably withheld,” and a reasonableness standard for ALAE. Carve-out for Aviva’s gross negligence/wilful misconduct.{quote}



{color:#ff5630}DR: Assuming the carveout is in place for Aviva’s gross negligence / wilful misconduct I don’t see an issue. The $2MM retention aggregate would be exhausted in a few months of normal operations which would then transfer the risk to Aviva. It would seem counter to their interests to inflate the payments/settlements to claimants?{color}

{quote}

# Double/stacked retentions if an occurrence spans policy years (Article IV). If Aviva pays under a renewal for the same occurrence, the agreement applies a separate retention per policy year - potentially double-dipping the SIR for one loss event. The alternative is to ask for SIR per claim/event or a cap.{quote}



{color:#ff5630}DR: {color}[~accountid:62a3287576c0360069f59b72]{color:#ff5630} correct me if i’m wrong here but this would only be in the event that there was a second claim for the same incident in the subsequent renewal year. A second claim in this case would have to fall under the subsequent renewal year policy as it goes by the date of the claim itself?{color}

{quote}

# Letter of Credit draw with no prior notice/cure and vague “failure to honour obligations” trigger. The alternative is to ask for draws only for undisputed, due amounts; written notice + 10–15 business-day cure; reconciliation/refund of any excess.{quote}



{color:#ff5630}DR: I think this could be worth being more specific. The LOC should only be converted under default or delinquency. I think the written notice and undisputed would be a reasonable addition?{color}

{quote}

# Default remedies are broad and cross-program (Article IX). A default “under this Agreement or any other” lets Aviva accelerate earned premium, cancel policies, and withhold Loss payments to Bolt or any affiliate - this is quite aggressive?{quote}

{color:#ff5630}DR: {color}[~accountid:62a3287576c0360069f59b72]{color:#ff5630} is this standard for insurance agreements?{color}



{quote}# Arbitration tilted toward insurer side (Article VI). Panel restricted to insurance executives; equitable (not legal) interpretation; no appeal; IBC President appoints on deadlock - risk of forum bias and limited recourse.{quote}

{color:#ff5630}DR: {color}[~accountid:62a3287576c0360069f59b72]{color:#ff5630} is this standard for insurance agreements?{color}

