Hi [~accountid:712020:13033e65-435c-4794-a380-e007eba7615a] - thanks

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
 In principle, I do not object to updating the basis to consent in this particular case given the regulatory context in Romania. I think it would be practical way to go forward, if the marketing team agrees in this specific case.

However, I would still defend the lawful basis of contract for the Image Release Form, and I would still liaise with [~accountid:712020:548c71a6-d1f4-404d-9c5e-482bc0de371a] re/ removing “consent” wording. I will outline my arguments below and I suggest to raise this on team call - this is indeed an interesting discussion, and I really value your input😌 
{panel}

{quote}* *‘Common sense’ tes*t{quote}

I would argue there is a benefit for the person in form of renumeration. But even if not, contract does not require monetary benefit to be binding. Here, the individual grants a licence and transfer of IP rights, and Bolt accepts specific obligations (attribution terms, etc.).

Image/IP release forms exist precisely because consent alone does not grant sufficient rights in copyright or neighbouring rights. The contract is giving Bolt enduring permission to exploit the image.

{quote}*Other regulatory guidance*{quote}

I believe the Irish and other guidelines address casual event photography.

By contrast, this form governs promotional content — commercially exploited. In this case, the processing of personal data (the individual’s image) is objectively necessary for Bolt to perform its contractual obligations (create, edit, publish promotional content using that likeness). I believe this fits within Article 6(1)(b) because the essence of the contract cannot be performed without that processing.

I also believe some regulators recognise this distinction for media production in guidelines - but I will need to dig deeper to find this.

{quote}*Utility re the RO Civil Code*{quote}

I think this is indeed a valid point in RO context, but's hard for me to imagine how this provision would apply in practice in talent agreements or media production. I think it can be deeper and there should be some legal interplay, i.e. maybe the individual can indeed object, but that doesn’t automatically void contractual commitments. 

In any case, for practically sake, I see no value in arguing to remove the right to withdraw permission if business is fine with it in this particular case.

