Hi [~accountid:712020:da3a34fc-4683-42dc-9868-0414839ff893], 

The Institute’s response is overall *positive* — it supports the idea of creating a “for women drivers” category, as long as we comply with the Belgian rules on *_positive actions_* (Loi Genre and the 2019 Royal Decree). To be noted that some parts of their letter discuss drivers’ employment status and reclassification risk, but as this is already being managed under Bolt’s existing mitigation work, it does not require further business action under the scope of this category.

+The Intitute identifies the conditions to be met in order to justify a positive action+: 

* specify and document the clear illegality between the number of female and male drivers registered  
on the Bolt platform;
* designate the elimination of this inequality as an objective;
* specify the concrete effect of the positive action;
* specify that the measure is temporary in nature, ending when the objective is achieved  
and no later than after a period of three years;
* demonstrate that it satisfies a proportionality test;
* to provide a guarantee that the positive action measure does not unnecessarily restrict the rights of others.

+In practice, we would recommend the following operational steps+: 

# *Quantify and evidence the inequality*
#* Pull data from Ops/Data on the current gender breakdown of active drivers (e.g. total vs female drivers, % change over last 12 months).
#* Extract available safety-related indicators (e.g. number of complaints or incidents reported by women passengers, satisfaction rates).
#* Present these in a short document to substantiate the “manifest inequality” required by Article 16 §2 of the _Loi Genre_.
# *Prepare a formal “Plan d’action positive” document + include a note to explain the motivation*
#* Structure it according to Article 6 of the 11 Feb 2019 Royal Decree:
#** identified inequality;
#** defined objective (increase female representation by X % in 3 years);
#** concrete measures (creation of category, awareness campaigns, targeted onboarding);
#** monitoring and end date (max 3 years);
#** proportionality statement and safeguard of others’ rights.
#* Draft a separate note referencing Article 15 of the _Code bruxellois de l’égalité,_ which includes examples of security concerns (e.g. harassment reports, safety-survey data) and explain that the measure:
#** pursues a _legitimate aim_ (women’s safety and sense of security);
#** is _necessary and proportionate_;
#** does _not reduce access_ for men since all other categories remain open.
# *Develop a monitoring and reporting mechanism*
#* Set yearly milestones (e.g. % increase in female drivers, feedback from women passengers).
#* Plan an annual internal review and a short report to Legal/HR to confirm the measure remains necessary and proportionate.
# *Communication and documentation*
#* Review internal and public messaging to ensure the measure is always framed as a _temporary, proportionate positive action_.


To summarise, if you can prepare a document to summarise all the data available (number of female drivers, number of incidents etc) we can have this available if an authority comes asking for it; in the meantime, we can help draft a letter of response to the Institute to thank them for their input and confirm that we will comply with the requirements.

Thank you

